cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use masque in published work, please cite it as below."
type: software
title: "masque: Synthetic Copies of Confidential Data Tables"
abstract: >-
  Creates a synthetic copy of a confidential data table, so that
  analysis code can be written and tested without the real values. By
  default, every outcome and covariate is simulated independently, with
  no link to other columns or to the real rows. Each numeric column
  keeps only its approximate range and shape. The experimental design
  and the pattern of missing values are kept exactly, and category
  labels, including those of the design and the treatments, are replaced
  by codes. Columns that look like identifiers or free text are left
  out. More of the real structure can be kept on request, at the cost of
  disclosing more about the real data. With mode = "local" (for the data
  owner's own use) or mode = "collaborate" (for sharing), numeric
  columns are simulated from a Gaussian copula, which approximately
  preserves each column's distribution and the correlations between
  them. In those modes, conditional = TRUE in mask() also preserves
  treatment effects. A folder of files or a multi-sheet workbook can be
  masked together. Keys shared by the tables get the same codes in every
  table, so the synthetic tables still join. Each run also returns a
  recipe, which maps the codes back to the real labels. The recipe is as
  confidential as the data and should stay with the data owner.
  apply_recipe() converts the real data into the names and codes of the
  copy, so code written on the copy runs on it unchanged, and unmask()
  converts results back to the real labels. The package gives no formal
  privacy guarantee.
authors:
